IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `intl-nouns`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for International Enforcement and Cooperation (2017), chapter 3: the
-- agencies' application of U.S. antitrust law to conduct involving foreign commerce.
--
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW. Chapter 3 "describes what connections to the United
-- States are sufficient for the Agencies to investigate or bring enforcement actions" (1
-- [pdf page 6]). The FTAIA's text is quoted by the guidelines [pdf pages 20-21]; this module
-- encodes the guidelines' account of it, not the statute. Every finding (direct, substantial,
-- reasonably foreseeable, adverse, proximately caused) is an input: whether an effect is
-- direct, substantial and reasonably foreseeable "is a question of fact" (3.2 [pdf page 24]).
--
-- The text reports three places where courts read the Act differently, and this module
-- records each as the text puts it, resolving none:
--   n.80 [pdf page 20]: whether Section 6a amended or codified the earlier law; the text says
--     both "likely yield the same results", and gives the agencies' view on a separate
--     showing (`n.80: the showing of a substantial and intended effect`).
--   n.82 [pdf page 21]: whether the FTAIA goes to the merits or to subject-matter jurisdiction;
--     the text says the difference "will not affect the Agencies' decisions", and the
--     not-proceed result names both.
--   n.93 [pdf page 24]: "direct" as proximate cause, or as "immediate consequence"; the text
--     takes proximate cause, so the input is the proximate-cause finding.

§ `Chapter 3: conduct involving foreign commerce`

§§ `3 How the FTAIA bears on each statute`

DECLARE `How the FTAIA bears on the statute (3)` IS ONE OF
    `Section 6a of the Sherman Act, added by the FTAIA, clarifies its application to conduct involving only non-import foreign commerce`
    `Section 5(a)(3) of the FTC Act, added by the FTAIA, closely parallels Section 6a of the Sherman Act`
    `the FTAIA did not address the reach of the Clayton Act, but the agencies would apply the principles of chapter 3 when making enforcement decisions on mergers and acquisitions involving trade or commerce with foreign nations`

-- 3 [pdf pages 20-21]; 2.1 [pdf page 7]; 2.2 [pdf page 9].
@ref 3 [pdf pages 20-21]; 2.1 [pdf page 7]; 2.2 [pdf page 9]
GIVEN s IS A `Statute the agencies enforce (3)`
GIVETH A `How the FTAIA bears on the statute (3)`
`3: how the FTAIA bears on` s MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF s EQUALS `the Sherman Act` THEN `Section 6a of the Sherman Act, added by the FTAIA, clarifies its application to conduct involving only non-import foreign commerce`
      IF s EQUALS `the FTC Act` THEN `Section 5(a)(3) of the FTC Act, added by the FTAIA, closely parallels Section 6a of the Sherman Act`
      OTHERWISE `the FTAIA did not address the reach of the Clayton Act, but the agencies would apply the principles of chapter 3 when making enforcement decisions on mergers and acquisitions involving trade or commerce with foreign nations`

§§ `3.1 Conduct involving import commerce`

-- 3.1 [pdf pages 22-23]: "the conduct being challenged must itself involve import commerce".
-- It does not apply "merely because those participating in the conduct are also engaged in
-- import commerce"; it "may apply to conduct even if the participants themselves do not act
-- as importers"; conduct "may 'involve' import commerce even if it is not directed
-- specifically or exclusively at import commerce and even if the import commerce involved
-- constitutes a relatively small portion of the worldwide commerce involved". So those four
-- facts are read by no rule. The sufficient patterns are the Examples': selling the goods
-- "in or for delivery to the United States" (Example A [pdf page 23]; n.88), and shipping
-- services "closely connected to the importation of goods into the United States" (Example B
-- [pdf page 23]); any other case is the agencies' finding (fork F1).
@ref 3.1 [pdf pages 22-23]; Illustrative Examples A and B [pdf pages 23-24]; n.87, n.88
GIVEN f IS A `Facts of the commerce the conduct involves (3)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `3.1: the challenged conduct itself involves U.S. import commerce` IF
        f's `the conduct involves trade or commerce with foreign nations`
    AND (    f's `the challenged conduct restrains the participants' sales of goods or services in or for delivery to the United States`
          OR f's `the conduct concerns services closely connected to the importation of goods into the United States`
          OR f's `the agencies find that the challenged conduct otherwise itself involves U.S. import commerce`)

-- 3.2 [pdf page 24]: non-import foreign commerce "means U.S. export commerce and wholly
-- foreign commerce". Conduct that involves import commerce is import commerce whatever else
-- it involves (fork F2).
@ref 3.1 [pdf page 22]; 3.2 [pdf page 24]; Illustrative Examples C and D [pdf pages 25, 27]
GIVEN f IS A `Facts of the commerce the conduct involves (3)`
GIVETH A `Kind of commerce the conduct involves (3)`
`3: the commerce the conduct involves` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF NOT f's `the conduct involves trade or commerce with foreign nations` THEN `commerce within the United States`
      IF `3.1: the challenged conduct itself involves U.S. import commerce` f THEN `U.S. import commerce`
      IF f's `the conduct involves U.S. export commerce` THEN `U.S. export commerce`
      OTHERWISE `wholly foreign commerce`

DECLARE `What chapter 3 says the FTAIA does with the conduct` IS ONE OF
    `the conduct is subject to the general requirements of the Sherman Act or the FTC Act for effects on commerce, not to the special requirements spelled out in the FTAIA`
    `the FTAIA initially places the conduct outside the reach of the Sherman Act and the FTC Act, unless its effects exception brings it back`

-- 3.1 [pdf page 22]: "conduct involving U.S. import commerce, like conduct involving commerce
-- within the United States, is 'subject to the Sherman Act's [or FTC Act's] general
-- requirements for effects on commerce, not to the special requirements spelled out in the
-- FTAIA.'" 3.2 [pdf page 24]: "The FTAIA initially places conduct involving non-import
-- foreign commerce ... outside the reach of the Sherman Act and FTC Act." Example A [pdf page
-- 23] says of import conduct that "Section 6a would not exempt this conspiracy".
@ref 3.1 [pdf page 22]; 3.2 [pdf page 24]; n.84 to n.86 [pdf page 22]; n.90 [pdf page 24]
GIVEN f IS A `Facts of the commerce the conduct involves (3)`
GIVETH A `What chapter 3 says the FTAIA does with the conduct`
`3: what the FTAIA does with the conduct` MEANS
    IF    `3: the commerce the conduct involves` f EQUALS `commerce within the United States`
       OR `3: the commerce the conduct involves` f EQUALS `U.S. import commerce`
    THEN `the conduct is subject to the general requirements of the Sherman Act or the FTC Act for effects on commerce, not to the special requirements spelled out in the FTAIA`
    ELSE `the FTAIA initially places the conduct outside the reach of the Sherman Act and the FTC Act, unless its effects exception brings it back`

§§ `3.2 Conduct involving non-import foreign commerce`

-- 3.2 [pdf pages 24-25]: the three requirements, each a finding. Any one found not to hold is
-- enough to say no; all three found to hold say yes; otherwise it is not yet determined.
@ref 3.2 [pdf pages 24-25]; n.93 to n.95 [pdf pages 24-25]
GIVEN e IS A `Effect of the conduct on U.S. commerce (3.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`3.2: the effect is direct, substantial, and reasonably foreseeable` MEANS
    IF    e's `the effect is proximately caused by the conduct` EQUALS JUST FALSE
       OR e's `the effect is substantial` EQUALS JUST FALSE
       OR e's `the effect is foreseeable to a reasonable person making practical business judgments` EQUALS JUST FALSE
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF     e's `the effect is proximately caused by the conduct` EQUALS JUST TRUE
            AND e's `the effect is substantial` EQUALS JUST TRUE
            AND e's `the effect is foreseeable to a reasonable person making practical business judgments` EQUALS JUST TRUE
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING

-- 3.2 [pdf pages 28-29]: what "gives rise to" requires depends on the claim. "In a damages
-- action brought under the antitrust laws, this provision requires that the effect on U.S.
-- commerce be an adverse one and that the effect proximately cause the plaintiff's
-- antitrust injury"; the United States suing under Section 4A of the Clayton Act "must
-- establish that the alleged conduct's effect on U.S. commerce proximately caused the
-- injury". For the agencies' civil actions for equitable relief and the Department's
-- criminal actions, "a direct, substantial, and reasonably foreseeable effect on U.S.
-- commerce would give rise to the sovereign's claim" (fork F3).
@ref 3.2 [pdf pages 28-29]; n.98 to n.101 [pdf pages 28-29]
GIVEN e IS A `Effect of the conduct on U.S. commerce (3.2)`
      c IS A `Claim (3.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`3.2: the effect gives rise to the claim` MEANS
    IF    c's `the kind of claim` EQUALS `a civil action for equitable relief brought by the agencies`
       OR c's `the kind of claim` EQUALS `a criminal enforcement action brought by the Department`
    THEN `3.2: the effect is direct, substantial, and reasonably foreseeable` e
    ELSE IF    e's `the effect is an adverse one` EQUALS JUST FALSE
            OR e's `the effect proximately caused the claimant's injury` EQUALS JUST FALSE
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF     e's `the effect is an adverse one` EQUALS JUST TRUE
                 AND e's `the effect proximately caused the claimant's injury` EQUALS JUST TRUE
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE NOTHING

-- An effect that meets both requirements of the effects exception for the claim.
@ref 3.2 [pdf pages 24, 28]; Section 6a(1) and (2) as quoted [pdf pages 20-21]
GIVEN e IS A `Effect of the conduct on U.S. commerce (3.2)`
      c IS A `Claim (3.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`3.2: the effect meets the effects exception for the claim` MEANS
    IF    `3.2: the effect is direct, substantial, and reasonably foreseeable` e EQUALS JUST FALSE
       OR `3.2: the effect gives rise to the claim` e c EQUALS JUST FALSE
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF     `3.2: the effect is direct, substantial, and reasonably foreseeable` e EQUALS JUST TRUE
            AND `3.2: the effect gives rise to the claim` e c EQUALS JUST TRUE
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING

GIVEN e IS A `Effect of the conduct on U.S. commerce (3.2)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the effect is on export commerce` MEANS
    e's `the commerce affected` EQUALS `the export trade or commerce of a person engaged in such trade or commerce in the United States`

DECLARE `Outcome of the FTAIA's effects exception (3.2)` IS ONE OF
    `the effects exception brings the conduct back within the reach of the Sherman Act and the FTC Act for this claim`
    `the effects exception is met only through the effect on export commerce, so the Acts apply only for injury to export business in the United States, which this claim is not for`
    `the effects exception does not bring the conduct within the reach of the Sherman Act and the FTC Act for this claim`

-- 3.2 [pdf page 24]: the effects exception "brings such conduct back within the reach of the
-- Acts if" the conduct has a qualifying effect "and that effect gives rise to a claim".
-- Section 6a's last sentence [pdf page 21]: if the Acts apply "only because of the operation
-- of paragraph (1)(B)" (the export effect), they apply "only for injury to export business in
-- the United States". One qualifying effect is enough. The export-only outcome needs every
-- other effect found to fail, not merely undetermined. Every effect found to fail, or no
-- effect named, is the exception not met (fork F19); otherwise NOTHING.
@ref 3.2 [pdf pages 24, 28-29]; Section 6a as quoted [pdf pages 20-21]; n.92 [pdf page 24]
GIVEN effects IS A LIST OF `Effect of the conduct on U.S. commerce (3.2)`
      c       IS A `Claim (3.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Outcome of the FTAIA's effects exception (3.2)`
`3.2: the effects exception` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF any (GIVEN e YIELD (NOT (`the effect is on export commerce` e)) AND `3.2: the effect meets the effects exception for the claim` e c EQUALS JUST TRUE) effects
      THEN JUST `the effects exception brings the conduct back within the reach of the Sherman Act and the FTC Act for this claim`
      IF     c's `the claim is for injury to export business in the United States`
         AND any (GIVEN e YIELD `the effect is on export commerce` e AND `3.2: the effect meets the effects exception for the claim` e c EQUALS JUST TRUE) effects
      THEN JUST `the effects exception brings the conduct back within the reach of the Sherman Act and the FTC Act for this claim`
      IF     any (GIVEN e YIELD `the effect is on export commerce` e AND `3.2: the effect meets the effects exception for the claim` e c EQUALS JUST TRUE) effects
         AND all (GIVEN e YIELD `the effect is on export commerce` e OR `3.2: the effect meets the effects exception for the claim` e c EQUALS JUST FALSE) effects
      THEN JUST `the effects exception is met only through the effect on export commerce, so the Acts apply only for injury to export business in the United States, which this claim is not for`
      IF all (GIVEN e YIELD `3.2: the effect meets the effects exception for the claim` e c EQUALS JUST FALSE) effects
      THEN JUST `the effects exception does not bring the conduct within the reach of the Sherman Act and the FTC Act for this claim`
      OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `3 What the agencies do`

DECLARE `What chapter 3 says the agencies do` IS ONE OF
    `a circumstance chapter 3 describes as a sufficient connection: if the agencies so determine, they generally will proceed in the normal course, subject to the considerations of chapter 4 and principles of prosecutorial discretion`
    `the agencies will not proceed: the FTAIA precludes the claim on the merits or strips the court of jurisdiction, and courts of appeals differ on which without affecting the agencies' decision`

-- 3 [pdf pages 19-20]: the chapter "describes circumstances under which a sufficient
-- connection exists. If the Agencies determine that a sufficient connection exists, the
-- Agencies generally will proceed in the normal course". The determination stays the
-- agencies', and the first result keeps it with them. n.82 [pdf page 21]:
-- "the Agencies will not proceed when the FTAIA precludes the claim on the merits or strips
-- the court of jurisdiction." For the agencies' own claims only (the equitable action, the
-- criminal action, the United States' damages action); a private plaintiff's claim is
-- NOTHING here. The circumstances are import commerce (3.1) and non-import commerce with
-- the effects exception met (3.2) (fork F4). Conduct involving no foreign commerce is
-- NOTHING: chapter 3 does not address it. 3.3 has its own, weaker, modal.
@ref 3 [pdf pages 19-20]; n.82 [pdf page 21]; 3.1 [pdf page 22]; 3.2 [pdf page 24]
GIVEN f       IS A `Facts of the commerce the conduct involves (3)`
      effects IS A LIST OF `Effect of the conduct on U.S. commerce (3.2)`
      c       IS A `Claim (3.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What chapter 3 says the agencies do`
`3: what the agencies do` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF c's `the kind of claim` EQUALS `a damages action brought by a private plaintiff`
      THEN NOTHING
      IF `3: the commerce the conduct involves` f EQUALS `commerce within the United States`
      THEN NOTHING
      IF `3: the commerce the conduct involves` f EQUALS `U.S. import commerce`
      THEN JUST `a circumstance chapter 3 describes as a sufficient connection: if the agencies so determine, they generally will proceed in the normal course, subject to the considerations of chapter 4 and principles of prosecutorial discretion`
      OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER `3.2: the effects exception` effects c
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN JUST o THEN
          IF o EQUALS `the effects exception brings the conduct back within the reach of the Sherman Act and the FTC Act for this claim`
          THEN JUST `a circumstance chapter 3 describes as a sufficient connection: if the agencies so determine, they generally will proceed in the normal course, subject to the considerations of chapter 4 and principles of prosecutorial discretion`
          ELSE JUST `the agencies will not proceed: the FTAIA precludes the claim on the merits or strips the court of jurisdiction, and courts of appeals differ on which without affecting the agencies' decision`

DECLARE `What section 3.2 says of a private damages claim` IS ONE OF
    `the plaintiff may recover damages for that injury`
    `the plaintiff cannot recover damages under the U.S. antitrust laws`

-- 3.2 [pdf page 28], an example "when anticompetitive conduct affects commerce around the
-- world": a plaintiff whose injury "arises from that conduct's effect on U.S. import commerce
-- may recover damages for that injury", but one "that suffers a foreign injury that is
-- independent of, and not proximately caused by, the conduct's effect on U.S. commerce
-- cannot recover damages under the U.S. antitrust laws." Each result is read on its own
-- sentence's terms only (fork F24): a private plaintiff, conduct involving foreign commerce,
-- and either an effect on U.S. import commerce found to have proximately caused the injury,
-- or a foreign injury that no effect on U.S. commerce is found to have proximately caused.
-- "May" is the text's. Every other case is NOTHING, the United States' claim under Section 4A
-- included: for it the text says only what the United States "must establish".
-- The example sits in the same paragraph as the effects exception's "gives rise to"
-- requirement (paragraph read in the PDF's layout, pdftotext -layout, page 28), so for
-- conduct that does not itself involve import commerce "may recover" also needs that effect
-- to meet the effects exception for this claim; while the exception is open for it, NOTHING
-- (corrected 2026-11-26 on the adversarial review, finding 1; NOTES.md section 14).
@ref 3.2 [pdf page 28]; n.98, n.99 [pdf page 28]
GIVEN f       IS A `Facts of the commerce the conduct involves (3)`
      effects IS A LIST OF `Effect of the conduct on U.S. commerce (3.2)`
      c       IS A `Claim (3.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 3.2 says of a private damages claim`
`3.2: what the text says of a private damages claim` MEANS
    IF     c's `the kind of claim` EQUALS `a damages action brought by a private plaintiff`
       AND f's `the conduct involves trade or commerce with foreign nations`
    THEN IF any (GIVEN e YIELD     e's `the commerce affected` EQUALS `U.S. import trade or commerce`
                              AND e's `the effect proximately caused the claimant's injury` EQUALS JUST TRUE
                              AND (   `3: the commerce the conduct involves` f EQUALS `U.S. import commerce`
                                   OR `3.2: the effect meets the effects exception for the claim` e c EQUALS JUST TRUE)) effects
         THEN JUST `the plaintiff may recover damages for that injury`
         ELSE IF     c's `the claimant's injury is a foreign injury`
                 AND all (GIVEN e YIELD e's `the effect proximately caused the claimant's injury` EQUALS JUST FALSE) effects
              THEN JUST `the plaintiff cannot recover damages under the U.S. antitrust laws`
              ELSE NOTHING
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `n.80 The earlier test of a substantial and intended effect`

DECLARE `What n.80 says of a substantial and intended effect` IS ONE OF
    `in the agencies' view, a separate showing of substantial and intended effects is unnecessary, because some of the challenged conduct takes place in the United States`
    `the conduct likely has a substantial and intended effect in the United States`

-- 3 [pdf page 20]: the antitrust laws "apply to foreign conduct that has a substantial and
-- intended effect in the United States". n.80 [pdf page 20]: courts differ on whether
-- Section 6a amended or codified that law; "If both ... apply in a single case, their
-- requirements likely yield the same results. Conduct that either involves U.S. import
-- commerce ... or has a direct, substantial, and reasonably foreseeable effect on U.S.
-- commerce ... likely has a substantial and intended effect in the United States. In the
-- Agencies' view, however, a separate showing of substantial and intended effects is
-- unnecessary when some of the challenged conduct takes place in the United States". The
-- agencies' view is read first; otherwise the "likely" sentence; otherwise NOTHING.
@ref 3 [pdf page 20]; n.78, n.80 [pdf page 20]
GIVEN f       IS A `Facts of the commerce the conduct involves (3)`
      effects IS A LIST OF `Effect of the conduct on U.S. commerce (3.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What n.80 says of a substantial and intended effect`
`n.80: the showing of a substantial and intended effect` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF f's `some of the challenged conduct takes place in the United States`
      THEN JUST `in the agencies' view, a separate showing of substantial and intended effects is unnecessary, because some of the challenged conduct takes place in the United States`
      IF    `3: the commerce the conduct involves` f EQUALS `U.S. import commerce`
         OR any (GIVEN e YIELD `3.2: the effect is direct, substantial, and reasonably foreseeable` e EQUALS JUST TRUE) effects
      THEN JUST `the conduct likely has a substantial and intended effect in the United States`
      OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `n.93 The reading of direct`

DECLARE `Reading of direct in Section 6a (n.93)` IS ONE OF
    `a reasonably proximate causal nexus`
    `an immediate consequence of the defendant's activity`

DECLARE `What n.93 says of a reading of direct` IS ONE OF
    `the reading the guidelines take: the proximate cause standard, which they say is more consistent with the language of the statute`
    `the reading one court of appeals has held; the guidelines say any difference between the two tests is unlikely to yield different results in the vast majority of cases`

-- 3.2 [pdf page 24] and n.93 [pdf pages 24-25]: the courts' split on "direct", reported as
-- the text reports it and resolved by nothing here (fork F13). The rules read the
-- proximate-cause finding, since that is the reading the text takes
-- (`the effect is proximately caused by the conduct`); this function only says what the text
-- says of each reading.
@ref 3.2 [pdf page 24]; n.93 [pdf pages 24-25]
GIVEN r IS A `Reading of direct in Section 6a (n.93)`
GIVETH A `What n.93 says of a reading of direct`
`n.93: what the text says of a reading of direct` r MEANS
    IF r EQUALS `a reasonably proximate causal nexus`
    THEN `the reading the guidelines take: the proximate cause standard, which they say is more consistent with the language of the statute`
    ELSE `the reading one court of appeals has held; the guidelines say any difference between the two tests is unlikely to yield different results in the vast majority of cases`

§§ `n.100 Restitution under the leniency policy`

DECLARE `What n.100 says of restitution to a victim` IS ONE OF
    `the leniency policy requires applicants to make restitution to the victims of their offense`
    `the Department construes the leniency policy not to require restitution to this victim`

-- n.100 [pdf page 29]: the policy "requires applicants to make restitution to the victims of
-- their offense", and "the Department construes the leniency policy to not require
-- restitution to victims whose antitrust injuries are independent of and not proximately
-- caused by an adverse effect on" domestic, import or U.S. exporters' export commerce,
-- "which effect was proximately caused by the anticompetitive activity being reported".
@ref n.100 [pdf page 29]
GIVEN v IS A `Victim of an offense reported under the leniency policy (n.100)`
GIVETH A `What n.100 says of restitution to a victim`
`n.100: restitution to a victim` MEANS
    IF     v's `the victim's antitrust injury is proximately caused by an adverse effect on trade or commerce within the United States, import trade or commerce, or the export trade or commerce of a person engaged in such trade or commerce in the United States`
       AND v's `that effect was proximately caused by the anticompetitive activity being reported`
    THEN `the leniency policy requires applicants to make restitution to the victims of their offense`
    ELSE `the Department construes the leniency policy not to require restitution to this victim`

§§ `3.3 Conduct involving U.S. government financing or purchasing`

DECLARE `What section 3.3 says of U.S. government interests` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies consider U.S. government interests to be sufficiently affected`
    `U.S. government interests would not be considered to be sufficiently implicated`

-- 3.3 [pdf page 30]: "The Agencies consider U.S. government interests to be sufficiently
-- affected when, as a result of its payment or financing, the U.S. government bears a
-- substantial portion of the cost of the transaction. U.S. government interests would not
-- be considered to be sufficiently implicated with respect to a transaction that is merely
-- funded by an international agency, or a transaction in which the foreign government
-- received non-earmarked funds from the United States as part of a general
-- government-to-government aid program." The text does not rank the two sentences, so a
-- transaction said to fall under an exclusion while the U.S. government bears a substantial
-- portion of its cost is contradictory input, and answers NOTHING (the coordinator's ruling
-- on contradictory inputs, 2026-11-26; fork F29; tests-independent.l4 line 738). Otherwise
-- an exclusion gives its passive, and the substantial portion gives the agencies' "consider".
-- Without either, the text states nothing (NOTHING), even for the three forms of
-- involvement it says the requisite involvement "could include" (fork F6).
@ref 3.3 [pdf pages 29-30]; n.102 [pdf pages 29-30]
GIVEN g IS A `Facts of U.S. government financing or purchasing (3.3)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 3.3 says of U.S. government interests`
`3.3: U.S. government interests` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF     (   g's `the involvement` EQUALS `funding of the transaction merely by an international agency`
              OR g's `the involvement` EQUALS `non-earmarked funds received by the foreign government from the United States as part of a general government-to-government aid program`)
         AND g's `as a result of its payment or financing, the U.S. government bears a substantial portion of the cost of the transaction`
      THEN NOTHING
      IF    g's `the involvement` EQUALS `funding of the transaction merely by an international agency`
         OR g's `the involvement` EQUALS `non-earmarked funds received by the foreign government from the United States as part of a general government-to-government aid program`
      THEN JUST `U.S. government interests would not be considered to be sufficiently implicated`
      IF g's `as a result of its payment or financing, the U.S. government bears a substantial portion of the cost of the transaction`
      THEN JUST `the agencies consider U.S. government interests to be sufficiently affected`
      OTHERWISE NOTHING

DECLARE `What section 3.3 says the agencies may do` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies may, in appropriate cases, take enforcement action`

-- 3.3 [pdf page 29]: "The Agencies may, in appropriate cases, take enforcement action when
-- the U.S. government is a purchaser, or substantially funds the purchase, of goods or
-- services for consumption or use abroad." Read with the test of when U.S. government
-- interests are sufficiently affected, which the PDF's layout (pdftotext -layout, pages 29-30)
-- prints in the same paragraph as the redress sentence and the requisite involvement (fork F6). "May, in appropriate cases" is
-- kept: it is weaker than chapter 3's "generally will proceed" (3 [pdf pages 19-20]).
@ref 3.3 [pdf pages 29-30]
GIVEN g IS A `Facts of U.S. government financing or purchasing (3.3)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 3.3 says the agencies may do`
`3.3: what the agencies may do` MEANS
    IF     g's `the U.S. government is a purchaser, or substantially funds the purchase, of goods or services for consumption or use abroad`
       AND `3.3: U.S. government interests` g EQUALS JUST `the agencies consider U.S. government interests to be sufficiently affected`
    THEN JUST `the agencies may, in appropriate cases, take enforcement action`
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `What section 3.3 says of redress` IS ONE OF
    `the case may qualify for redress under the federal antitrust laws`

-- 3.3 [pdf page 29]: "Cases in which the effect of anticompetitive conduct with respect to
-- the sale of these goods or services falls primarily on U.S. taxpayers may qualify for
-- redress under the federal antitrust laws." The redress sentence, the requisite involvement,
-- the substantial-portion test and both exclusions are one paragraph in the PDF's layout
-- (pdftotext -layout, pages 29-30), so the redress sentence is read with the test: it
-- answers only when U.S. government interests are sufficiently affected; under an exclusion,
-- or without the substantial portion, NOTHING (corrected 2026-11-26 on the adversarial
-- review, finding 2; NOTES.md section 14).
@ref 3.3 [pdf page 29]; n.102 [pdf pages 29-30]
GIVEN g IS A `Facts of U.S. government financing or purchasing (3.3)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 3.3 says of redress`
`3.3: whether the case may qualify for redress` MEANS
    IF     g's `the U.S. government is a purchaser, or substantially funds the purchase, of goods or services for consumption or use abroad`
       AND g's `the effect of the conduct with respect to the sale of the goods or services falls primarily on U.S. taxpayers`
       AND `3.3: U.S. government interests` g EQUALS JUST `the agencies consider U.S. government interests to be sufficiently affected`
    THEN JUST `the case may qualify for redress under the federal antitrust laws`
    ELSE NOTHING
